# Line endings.
#
# This checkout runs with `core.autocrlf=true`, and the committed blobs were a
# mix of CRLF and LF — `lkh2/src/adapter.rs` was mixed *within a single file*
# (89 of 352 lines LF). `rustfmt` rewrites a whole file to LF the moment it
# touches one line, so a three-line change would arrive as a thousand-line diff.
# That cost real review time repeatedly, and more than once a genuine change was
# nearly lost inside the churn.
#
# `text=auto` lets git keep detecting binary content on its own; `eol=lf` pins
# both the committed blob and the working tree to LF, so `core.autocrlf` no
# longer has anything to disagree with. Rust, cargo and rustfmt are all
# indifferent to line endings on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces: never let the rule above touch a format where a stray byte
# would corrupt the file. `text=auto` should already detect these, but these are
# exactly the files where being wrong is expensive.
*.pcapng binary
*.pcap   binary
*.der    binary
*.p12    binary
*.pfx    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
